From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maicon Alves <alvesmaiconn@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 21:00:46 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 46/47] client: gliding with an elytra as in Minecraft
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Minecraft's elytra had nothing to stand on: a game could only fake a glide with the gravity and
speed overrides, which neither follow the look nor keep Minecraft's speeds.

The new physics override glide lets the player glide. Jump pressed again in the air, out of a
liquid, not climbing, flying or attached, starts it, as Minecraft 26.3's LocalPlayer.aiStep and
Player.tryToStartFallFlying do; the ground, a liquid, a ladder, flying or the server taking the
override away ends it. Gliding, the client moves the player with Minecraft's
LivingEntity.updateFallFlyingMovement a 20 Hz tick at a time, the speed constant between ticks,
so the player is where Minecraft's would be at every tick at any frame rate: 0.08 a tick down
less 75 % of it times cos² of the pitch, a fall turned forward along the look, looking up trading
speed for height (3.2 times what it takes), the horizontal speed turned 10 % a tick toward the
look, then 99 % of it kept and 98 % of the vertical. The gravity override scales the 0.08.

The new override glide_boost starts a firework rocket's boost of that many ticks, each set a new
one (an id the server counts up, so two rockets of the same length both count): each tick the
speed goes half way to 1.5 blocks a tick along the look, and 0.1 more, as FireworkRocketEntity.tick
pulls the player it is attached to; its ticks also go by while not gliding, as the rocket burns on.

A wall hit while gliding hurts as Minecraft's handleFallFlyingCollisions: 10 a block a tick of
horizontal speed the wall takes away, less 3, rounded; not the edge of unloaded blocks, where
Minecraft's player just waits. The fall is Minecraft's from the glide on (what fell before it
taken from the speed, as the air gives it): every block gone down, back to 1 while the glide goes
down slower than half a block a tick (Entity.checkFallDistanceAccumulation), so a gentle landing
does no damage and a dive does as a fall from where it began; water, ladders, flying and a
teleport end it. Landed, Minecraft's ground friction (54.6 % kept a tick) takes the glide's speed
down to walking; stopped in the air, Minecraft's air drags it.

The client tells the server with a new control, glide (bit 10, in get_player_control and the
CSM's get_control), for the game's pose, wings and wear; the movement anticheat lets a player
that may glide and does through.

Checked in the native build, jump pressed by xdotool, the position and speed logged every frame
and set against Minecraft's ticks simulated from the same start (Minecraft in brackets, m/s
horizontal and vertical): level flight after 15 s 29.27 and -2.99 (29.26, -2.99); 15° down after
10 s 32.60 and -3.83 (32.57, -3.83); 60° down after 6 s 43.87 and -27.87 (43.65, -27.87); level
then 30° up, 2 s on 9.32 and +7.08 (9.05, +6.80); rockets in a row at level flight 33.45 and -0.63
(33.45, -0.63), 30° up 27.85 and +18.51 (27.85, +18.51); level flight at 15 FPS after 15 s 29.36
(29.36). A wall hit at 1.39 blocks a tick took 11 HP (10.98), a dive landing after 14.2 blocks of
fall 12 (12), a landing at 5° and one into water none (none).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

